WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.548
Start with a push deploy.

2
00:00:04.548 --> 00:00:08.396
Your CI pipeline tests the code and builds the image.

3
00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:10.647
Its last step is kubectl apply.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.879
Something outside the cluster sends the change in.

5
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:18.332
So CI must hold a key that can write to the cluster.

6
00:00:18.332 --> 00:00:22.163
Any workflow that can read that secret can change production.

7
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.588
And the deploy only happens while the pipeline runs.

8
00:00:25.588 --> 00:00:29.344
If someone edits the cluster an hour later, nothing notices.

9
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:32.308
GitOps closes that gap from the other side.

10
00:00:32.308 --> 00:00:35.406
Git becomes the only place where changes happen.

11
00:00:35.406 --> 00:00:37.148
It holds the desired state.

12
00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:42.519
A program inside the cluster keeps the cluster equal to git, all the time.

13
00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:44.621
Here, that program is Argo CD.

14
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.910
Argo CD pulls.

15
00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.080
It reads git, and applies what it finds.

16
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:55.417
It also watches the live objects it manages, so it sees any change in the cluster within seconds.

17
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.670
CI still tests the code and builds the image.

18
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.422
Then it changes a file in git, and that is all CI does.

19
00:01:02.422 --> 00:01:04.537
It needs no cluster key at all.

20
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:07.033
And git log becomes your deploy log.

21
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:09.333
It shows who changed what, and when.

22
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:16.111
Argo CD keeps comparing two things: the desired state from git, and the live state in the cluster.

23
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:20.071
When every object it manages matches git, the app is Synced.

24
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:24.771
A second status, health, answers a different question: does it work?

25
00:01:24.771 --> 00:01:25.699
Here, Healthy.

26
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:28.285
Now a teammate fixes something by hand.

27
00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:32.067
They change the greeting in the live ConfigMap, and skip git.

28
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:33.948
Argo CD sees it at once.

29
00:01:33.948 --> 00:01:37.592
Live no longer matches git, so the app is OutOfSync.

30
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:41.061
But it is still Healthy, because the app still runs fine.

31
00:01:41.061 --> 00:01:43.571
Sync and health are two separate questions.

32
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:46.637
By default, Argo CD only reports this drift.

33
00:01:46.637 --> 00:01:51.917
Undoing it is a setting called self-heal, and it stays off until you turn it on.

34
00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:57.174
With self-heal on, Argo CD syncs git's version back after a short pause.

35
00:01:57.174 --> 00:01:58.309
You did nothing.

36
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.364
To change the greeting for good, change git.

37
00:02:01.364 --> 00:02:03.472
Edit the file, commit, and push.

38
00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:08.393
Argo CD pulls the new commit, and finds that the cluster differs.

39
00:02:08.393 --> 00:02:09.120
OutOfSync.

40
00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:11.033
So it applies the difference.

41
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.227
Synced again, with the new greeting.

42
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.747
Now say that commit was a mistake.

43
00:02:15.747 --> 00:02:18.508
With GitOps, a rollback is a commit too.

44
00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:19.820
You run git revert.

45
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.100
It makes a new commit that undoes the bad one.

46
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.515
History stays intact.

47
00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:28.129
Push it, and Argo CD deploys the state from before.

48
00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:30.219
Why not kubectl rollout undo?

49
00:02:30.219 --> 00:02:36.005
It changes the cluster but not git, so self-heal would put the bad version straight back.

50
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.686
Git holds what you want.

51
00:02:38.686 --> 00:02:44.587
Argo CD, inside the cluster, pulls it, compares, and keeps the cluster equal to git.
